===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷʰer-}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|warm}}, {{m|enm|werm}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wearm}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*warm}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*warmaz}}, with different proposed origins:
# {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷʰer-||warm, hot}}, related to {{cog|grc|θερμός}}, {{cog|la|formus}}, {{cog|sa|घर्म}}.
# {{der|en|ine-pro|*wer-||to burn}}, related to {{cog|hit|tr=warnuzi}} and to {{cog|cu|варити}}.
The dispute is due to differing opinions on how initial [[Proto-Indo-European]] {{m|ine-pro||*gʷʰ-}} evolved in Germanic: some think that {{m|ine-pro||*gʷʰ}} would have turned to {{m|gem-pro||*b}}, and that the root {{m|ine-pro||*gʷʰer-}} would instead have given rise to {{m|en|burn}} etc. Some have also proposed a merger of the two roots.

