====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make or keep [[warm#Adjective|warm]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Isaiah 44|15|passage=Then shall it [an ash tree] be for a man to burn; for he will take thereof and '''warm''' himself.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|author=w:Henry Wadsworth Longfellow|title=Morituri Salutamus
|passage=enough to '''warm''', but not enough to burn}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become warm, to heat up.
#: {{ux|en|My socks are '''warming''' by the fire.}}
#: ''The earth soon '''warms''' on a clear summer day.''
# {{indtr|en|to}} {{q|sometimes in the form [[warm up]]}} To [[favour]] [[increasingly]].
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=5|passage=Mr. Campion appeared suitably impressed and she '''warmed''' to him. He was very easy to talk to with those long clown lines in his pale face, a natural goon, born rather too early she suspected.}}
#: {{ux|en|He is '''warming''' to the idea.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her classmates are gradually '''warming''' to her.}}
# {{lb|en|[[Appendix:Glossary#ditransitive|ditransitive]] with '''''[[to]]'''''}} To cause (someone) to favour (something) increasingly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Matt Wray|title=Not Quite White|page=xi|text=It is with no small degree of irony that I confess that immersing myself in an interdisciplinary project has '''warmed''' me to the seductions of disciplinary perspectives.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[ardent]] or [[animated]].
#: ''The speaker '''warms''' as he proceeds.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make engaged or earnest; to interest; to engage; to excite ardor or zeal in; to enliven.
#* '''1717''' November 20, {{w|Alexander Pope}}, letter to the Bishop of Rochester
#*: there was a collection of all that had been written{{...}}: I '''warmed''' my head with them.
#* {{RQ:Keble Christian Year
|passage=Bright hopes, that erst the bosom '''warmed'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give [[emotional]] [[warmth]] to a person.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Right Life: Or, Candid Talks On Vital Themes|author=Joseph Augustus Seiss|year=1886|text=That is just the way God tells me this book is His Word. I read it, and it '''warms''' me and gives me light.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[beat]] or [[spank]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1945|title=The Atlantic|volume=176|page=94
|passage=Not bothering to turn around and not missing a mouthful, Myrtle comforted her with threats of "I'll '''warm''' your bottom"; "I'll turn you over to your dad"; "I'll lock you in the truck"; "I'll send for the bogey man" — all of which Darleen ignored {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[scold]] or [[abuse]] verbally.
# {{lb|en|computing|transitive}} To [[prepopulate]] (a [[cache]]) so that its contents are ready for other [[user]]s.

